WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.590
My phone broke. Are my coins smashed too?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.027
Your coins aren't inside your phone.

00:00:11.027 --> 00:00:14.975
A wallet lets you see assets on the blockchain and uses keys to approve actions.

00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:19.170
Whether you can recover depends on the wallet type and the recovery options you kept.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.787
With a custodial exchange account, the platform mainly manages the assets and keys,

00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:38.591
and you act through your account.

00:00:38.591 --> 00:00:41.653
With a self-custody wallet, you control the credentials.

00:00:41.653 --> 00:00:44.550
Convenience and responsibility are split differently.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.560
Forget your password on either one, and you just call support?

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.284
Don't think of it that way. With a standard recovery-phrase wallet,

00:01:07.284 --> 00:01:10.503
if you lose the phrase and have no other valid recovery method,

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.240
support can't get it back for you.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.945
Keeping assets on a platform carries platform risk.

00:01:26.945 --> 00:01:30.855
Holding them yourself can go wrong through a leaked key or a bad approval.

00:01:30.855 --> 00:01:35.240
Today, just understand the difference. You don't need to move everything right now.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.794
Who controls the permission to move the assets? Answer that first.

00:01:46.794 --> 00:01:49.210
Then decide which account or wallet you need.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:01.210
Two doors look the same. One is fake. Next episode: how to spot the real one.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.740
Understand first, then act.
